{#
  GALLERY, the work as a sequence of pictures.

  BUILT ON THE KOLENDA "SELECTED WORK" PAGE, the garden-design site the client
  set as the reference for this build (bartoszkolenda.com/work). That page is
  a text-led header with a scroll cue, then one sequential run of large image
  cards on the dark ground, each captioned with a title and a year, closed by
  the studio's figures and client logos. Nothing is explained; the pictures
  are the argument and the captions only say where you are.

  WHAT THAT BECOMES HERE. Every job is one wide, cinematic lead frame on navy
  with a numbered caption beneath it (01, the name, the place), and then its
  other photographs run on under it at single size. No job headers, no notes,
  no bento: the first pass of this page grouped and explained, and the client
  did not like it. The run ends on the site's own stats band and client marquee,
  as Kolenda's does, and the footer carries the enquiry.

  DATA comes from _data/photos.js: names, places, alt text, and which picture
  leads each job. Add a photograph there and nothing changes here. The lead
  frame takes the full 1536px file; the run beneath takes the 960px copies in
  /assets/gallery/, which is all a third-width card needs.

  THE LEAD FRAME DRIFTS on scroll in the same `.parallax` frame every other
  photograph on the site sits in, so the page moves the way the service bands
  do rather than as a static wall.

  NAMES follow the case-study rule: initials as the client supplied them,
  places at town or county level. See the note in photos.js on U Farm.
#}
    <!-- ===================== HERO (dark) ===================== -->
    <section class="hero gallery-hero">
      <div class="container hero__inner">
        <p class="hero__eyebrow">Gallery</p>
        {# WRITTEN TO THE COPYWRITING SKILL (coreyhaines31/marketingskills), from
           the full file this time, with the client's own words as the source.

           CONTEXT the skill asks for first. Page: gallery, proof. One action:
           book a site survey. Audience: owners of estates and country houses
           with a private supply, often arriving after a system someone else
           built has let them down (the Woking testimonial: "continually
           breaking down"; the U Farm, RF Estate and B Farm case studies all
           began that way). Their language, from the three real testimonials:
           "doing a job properly", "from the borehole pump right through to
           the taps", "complete confidence", "attention to detail".

           HEADLINE. Both halves are the Elstead client's own phrases. "Now you
           can" test: now you can see a job done properly, borehole to tap, in
           Invicta's own photographs. Compelling, and true of every frame on
           the page. Swap test: a competitor running stock photography cannot
           say "in our own photographs". Long first line, short italic second,
           the cut every hero on this site uses; the only candidate of seven
           that held two lines at 1180px with the first the longer.

           SUBHEAD, on the skill's three beats: the discomfort (a system that
           kept breaking down, in the customer's words), the vision (how we
           leave a supply), and the path is the first photograph, one scroll
           down. A CTA pair sat under the lead for a while; the client took it
           out so the pictures start higher. The nav's "Send enquiry" and the
           footer carry the ask.

           Self-check run: no contrast reveal, no negation list, no colon or
           question reveal, no trailing pile-on, no em dash, no exclamation. #}
        <h1 class="hero__title">Done properly, borehole to tap,<br><span class="em">in our own photographs.</span></h1>
        <div class="hero__scroll" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="hero__scroll-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 4v15M6 13l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </div>
        {# COPY, per the copywriting skill the client pointed at (coreyhaines31/
           marketingskills): one claim per sentence, specific over vague, no
           fragments or triple lists doing the work of a sentence, and a CTA that
           says what the visitor gets rather than "enquire". The count line that
           used to sit here ("49 photographs from 15 jobs") is gone at the
           client's request. #}
        <div class="hero__lead">
          <p class="hero__lead-lg">Many of these jobs began as another firm’s system that kept breaking down. Every photograph is from a supply we designed, installed or look after, and this is how we leave them.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ===================== THE RUN (dark) =====================
      EVERY ROW IS FULL. A job's photographs are dealt into rows that always
      close: a pair at two-thirds and one-third (the narrow one cropped to
      portrait so the pair shares a height), the next pair mirrored, or a row
      of three. The first pass left a one-third hole whenever a job had one
      photograph over, and the client asked for no blank space and for rows
      that are not always 50/50. How a job is dealt, by its count:
        1  one full frame at 2:1
        2  the lead at two-thirds, the other beside it at one-third
        3+ the lead full width at 2:1, then the rest: a row of three first if
           the rest is an odd number, then pairs, alternating sides
      The caption (numeral, name, place) sits under the first row.
    -->
    <section class="section gallery" aria-label="Photographs by job">
      <div class="container">
        {% for job in photos %}
        {% if job.photos and job.photos.length %}
        {% set lead = null %}
        {% for p in job.photos %}{% if p.lead and not lead %}{% set lead = p %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
        {% if not lead %}{% set lead = job.photos[0] %}{% endif %}
        {% set rest = [] %}
        {% for p in job.photos %}{% if p.src != lead.src %}{% set _ = rest.push(p) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
        {% set n = job.photos.length %}
        <article class="gallery-job" id="{{ job.id }}" aria-labelledby="gallery-job-{{ job.id }}">

          {% if n == 2 %}
          {# Two photographs: one row, the lead wide and the other narrow. #}
          <ul class="gallery__row gallery__row--pair" role="list">
            <li class="gallery__cell gallery__cell--wide">
              <figure class="gallery__card">
                <div class="gallery__frame parallax">
                  <img src="{{ lead.src }}" alt="{{ lead.alt }}" width="1536" height="1024" loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}" decoding="async">
                </div>
              </figure>
            </li>
            <li class="gallery__cell gallery__cell--narrow">
              <figure class="gallery__card">
                <div class="gallery__frame">
                  <img src="{{ rest[0].src | replace('/assets/', '/assets/gallery/') }}" alt="{{ rest[0].alt }}" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                </div>
              </figure>
            </li>
          </ul>
          {% else %}
          <figure class="gallery__card gallery__lead">
            <div class="gallery__frame parallax">
              <img src="{{ lead.src }}" alt="{{ lead.alt }}" width="1536" height="1024" loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}" decoding="async">
            </div>
          </figure>
          {% endif %}

          <div class="gallery__caption">
            <span class="gallery__index numeral" aria-hidden="true">{{ "0" if loop.index < 10 }}{{ loop.index }}</span>
            <h2 class="gallery__name" id="gallery-job-{{ job.id }}">{{ job.name }}</h2>
            {% if job.place %}<span class="gallery__place">{{ job.place }}</span>{% endif %}
          </div>

          {% if n >= 3 %}
          {#- Deal the rest into full rows: a trio first when the count is odd,
              then pairs, alternating which side carries the wide picture. -#}
          {% set r = rest.length %}
          {% set i = 0 %}
          {% set k = 0 %}
          {% if r % 2 == 1 %}
          <ul class="gallery__row gallery__row--trio" role="list">
            {% for p in rest.slice(0, 3) %}
            <li class="gallery__cell">
              <figure class="gallery__card"><div class="gallery__frame">
                <img src="{{ p.src | replace('/assets/', '/assets/gallery/') }}" alt="{{ p.alt }}" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              </div></figure>
            </li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
          {% set i = 3 %}
          {% endif %}
          {% for step in range(0, 20) %}
          {% if i < r %}
          <ul class="gallery__row gallery__row--pair{% if k % 2 == 1 %} gallery__row--mirror{% endif %}" role="list">
            <li class="gallery__cell gallery__cell--wide">
              <figure class="gallery__card"><div class="gallery__frame">
                <img src="{{ rest[i].src | replace('/assets/', '/assets/gallery/') }}" alt="{{ rest[i].alt }}" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              </div></figure>
            </li>
            <li class="gallery__cell gallery__cell--narrow">
              <figure class="gallery__card"><div class="gallery__frame">
                <img src="{{ rest[i + 1].src | replace('/assets/', '/assets/gallery/') }}" alt="{{ rest[i + 1].alt }}" width="960" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
              </div></figure>
            </li>
          </ul>
          {% set i = i + 2 %}
          {% set k = k + 1 %}
          {% endif %}
          {% endfor %}
          {% endif %}
        </article>
        {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ THE FIGURES, THEN WHO TRUSTS US ============
      The same close as Kolenda's work page, in the site's own components: the
